%I A401490 #12 Nov 04 2026 22:08:59
%S A401490 1,4,12,30,60,180,210,420,1260,6300,44100,3063060,9699690,19399380,
%T A401490 58198140,30808063,123232252,369696756,924241890,1848483780,
%U A401490 5545451340,955049953,3820199812,11460599436,28651498590,57302997180,171908991540,200560490130,35336848261
%N A401490 a(n) is the smallest integer with exactly n divisors that are brilliant (A078972).
%C A401490 First 10 terms agree with those of A220264; then a(10) = 44100, whereas A220264(10) = 2310.
%C A401490 Often a(n+1) = k * a(n) for some integer k (same as A220264).
%C A401490 About a(15) = 30808063 = 11 * 13 * 17 * 19 * 23 * 29, product of six 2-digits primes, that produce binomial(6,2) = 15 brilliant divisors.
%C A401490 About a(28) = 35336848261 = 11 * 13 * 17 * 19 * 23 * 29 * 31 * 37, product of eight 2-digits primes, that produce binomial(8,2) = 28 brilliant divisors.
%H A401490 Giovanni Resta, <a href="https://www.numbersaplenty.com/set/brilliant_number/">Brilliant numbers</a>, Numbers Aplenty.
%e A401490 Of the 18 divisors of 180, five are brilliant numbers: {4, 6, 9, 10, 15}, and there is no integer < 180 with 5 such brilliant divisors.
%Y A401490 Cf. A078972, A401489.
%Y A401490 Cf. A220264 (semiprime).
%K A401490 nonn,base,new
%O A401490 0,2
%A A401490 _Bernard Schott_, Oct 29 2026
